'''''Rumex scutatus''''' (syn. ''Rumex alpestris'') is a plant in the [[Polygonaceae|buckwheat family]], used as a [[culinary herb]]. Its common names include '''French sorrel''',<ref>{{PLANTS|id=RUSC8|taxon=Rumex scutatus|accessdate=26 October 2015 }}</ref> '''buckler sorrel''', '''shield-leaf sorrel''', and sometimes the [[culinary name]] "green-sauce".<ref>Robert Hooper, ''Lexicon medicum'', 1829 [https://books.google.com/books?id=zWBZAAAAIAAJ&pg=PA253&dq=rumex%20scutatus ''s.v.'' ''Rumex scutatus'']</ref>

As a culinary herb, it is used in salads, soups, and sauces (especially for fish).<ref>Robert Pincus, "Pucker Up", ''[[Gourmet (magazine)|Gourmet]]'', May 28, 2008 [http://www.gourmet.com.s3-website-us-east-1.amazonaws.com/food/2008/05/sorrel.html] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241211132118/http://www.gourmet.com.s3-website-us-east-1.amazonaws.com/food/2008/05/sorrel.html |date=2024-12-11 }}</ref> French sorrel tastes [[Sour|tart]] from its [[oxalic acid]] content, with a hint of lemon.<ref>''The New Oxford Book of Food Plants'', p 199, J. G. Vaughn and C. Geissler, OUP, Oxford, 1997, {{ISBN|0-19-854825-7}}.</ref> Later in the season, it can be [[Bitter (taste)|bitter]].<ref>Tommy Werner, "What's the Score on Sorrel?", ''Epicurious'' https://www.epicurious.com/ingredients/what-is-sorrel-recipes-article</ref>

French sorrel is [[Hardiness (plants)|hardy]] in most regions, tolerating frost, full sun and short dry spells.<ref>[http://www.jekkasherbfarm.com/details_new.asp?productid=328 Welcome to Jekka's Herb Farm |Specialising in Organic, Culinary, Aromatic, Decorative and Medicinal Herbs]</ref> It grows quickly to a clump up to {{cvt|1|m|ft}} in diameter, with long leaves up to {{cvt|10|cm|in|0}} wide. It is sometimes preferred for culinary uses to ''[[Rumex acetosa]]'', common sorrel.<ref>{{citation |url=https://www.pfaf.org/user/plant.aspx?LatinName=Rumex+scutatus |title=Plants for a Future |access-date=13 May 2016 }}</ref>

